Subject: [PATCH] HID: asus: do not abort probe when not necessary

This is a BACKPORT of an upstream commit. It is not new work:

  commit 7253091766ded0fd81fe8d8be9b8b835495b06e8
  "HID: asus: do not abort probe when not necessary"
  Denis Benato <denis.benato@linux.dev>, 2026-02-28
  Fixes: d3af6ca9a8c3 ("HID: asus: fix UAF via HID_CLAIMED_INPUT validation")

The commit it fixes, d3af6ca9a8c3, IS carried by linux-6.18.10; the fix
is not, so 6.18.10 ships the defect with none of the correction.

WHAT GOES WRONG. asus_probe checks, after hid_hw_start, that an input
device was registered, and treats its absence as a probe failure for the
whole HID interface:

	if (!drvdata->input || !(hdev->claimed & HID_CLAIMED_INPUT)) {
		hid_err(hdev, "Asus input not registered\n");
		ret = -ENOMEM;
		goto err_stop_hw;
	}

That guard was added to prevent a use-after-free: when no usages are
mapped, the input devices are freed during registration and
drvdata->input is left pointing at freed memory. Not dereferencing that
pointer is correct. Failing the probe is not.

An ASUS N-KEY keyboard presents four HID interfaces. Three carry mappable
usages and bind normally. The fourth declares a single vendor collection
on usage page 0xFF82, and hid-asus maps only pages 0xFF31
(HID_UP_ASUSVENDOR) and 0xFF00 (HID_UP_MSVENDOR), so every usage on that
interface falls through asus_input_mapping's final "return 0" and the HID
core creates no input device for it. Having no input device is the normal
and expected state for a vendor-only interface, and the guard turns it
into a failure.

The consequences, measured on a laptop carrying this keyboard, on every
boot in the journal:

  * "probe with driver asus failed with error -12" once per boot, on the
    vendor interface.
  * err_stop_hw calls hid_hw_stop, which tears down the hiddev and hidraw
    nodes hid_hw_start had just created. The interface is left with no
    driver bound, no input device and no hidraw node, so the vendor
    control channel the hardware offers is unreachable from user space.
  * -12 is -ENOMEM. Nothing here allocated or failed to allocate, so the
    reported cause is misleading as well as the outcome being wrong.

WHAT THE UPSTREAM FIX DOES. It keeps the NULL/claimed check but uses it
to guard the two places that USE drvdata->input, rather than to abort:
an interface with no input device names nothing, starts no multitouch,
and returns success, so it stays bound and keeps its hidraw node. The
use-after-free the original guard existed to prevent remains impossible,
because the pointer is still never dereferenced when it may be stale.

The upstream hunk applies to 6.18.10 with a line offset only; upstream's
hid-asus.c has since diverged substantially (roughly 300 lines of later
work), so this backport carries that one hunk rather than the file.

Decided 2026-08-26: adopt the upstream commit rather than author a local
variant, so this diverges from 6.18.10 only where upstream already has,
and the declaration drops out when the kernel moves to a version carrying
the fix.

diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-asus.c b/drivers/hid/hid-asus.c
index fa9a24f279f49..26047ccc8e7da 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-asus.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-asus.c
@@ -1324,22 +1324,17 @@ static int asus_probe(struct hid_device *hdev, const struct hid_device_id *id)
 	 * were freed during registration due to no usages being mapped,
 	 * leaving drvdata->input pointing to freed memory.
 	 */
-	if (!drvdata->input || !(hdev->claimed & HID_CLAIMED_INPUT)) {
-		hid_err(hdev, "Asus input not registered\n");
-		ret = -ENOMEM;
-		goto err_stop_hw;
-	}
-
-	if (drvdata->tp) {
-		drvdata->input->name = "Asus TouchPad";
-	} else {
-		drvdata->input->name = "Asus Keyboard";
-	}
+	if (drvdata->input && (hdev->claimed & HID_CLAIMED_INPUT)) {
+		if (drvdata->tp)
+			drvdata->input->name = "Asus TouchPad";
+		else
+			drvdata->input->name = "Asus Keyboard";
 
-	if (drvdata->tp) {
-		ret = asus_start_multitouch(hdev);
-		if (ret)
-			goto err_stop_hw;
+		if (drvdata->tp) {
+			ret = asus_start_multitouch(hdev);
+			if (ret)
+				goto err_stop_hw;
+		}
 	}
 
 	return 0;
